-- Sharing metadata.  Read by tooling and the manager's detail pane; never by
-- the loader's merge, so nothing here can break a load.
return {
  summary = "Form changes: mega evolution, Dynamax and Gigantamax, Terastallization, Z-Moves, primal reversion for Groudon and Kyogre, condition-driven forms for eight more species, persistent held-item forms for Rotom, Giratina, Palkia, Dialga, Zacian, Zamazenta, Shaymin, Arceus (its seventeen Plates), Silvally (its seventeen Memories) and Genesect (its four Drives, purely cosmetic), fusion for Kyurem, Necrozma and Calyrex, which joins two Pokemon into one and keeps the partner in the PC until they are separated again, and Ultra Burst for a Necrozma already fused into Dusk Mane or Dawn Wings, which becomes Ultra Necrozma for the rest of the battle.",
  author = "sanjinpepic",
  contact = "https://github.com/sanjinpepic",
  tags = { "gameplay", "battle" },
  differences = {
    changed = {
      "a fifth entry on the battle menu that hosts the transformations the player has to ask for and shows whichever are available; it reads MEGA when the trainer has a Key Stone and the active mon carries a matching stone, and DYNAMAX for any mon at all once the trainer has a Dynamax Band, and LEFT and RIGHT cycle between them when both are on offer, which the cell marks with a small hollow arrow at the right-hand end of its row so the second one is never hidden behind the first",
      "an existing save loses mega evolution until a Key Stone is bought, and loses Dynamax until a Dynamax Band is: both were previously ungated, and the real games require the trainer to carry one as well as the Pokemon to carry its own item",
      "the menu cell also reads TERA once the trainer has a Tera Orb, which every species may use whatever it is carrying",
      "and Z-MOVE once the trainer has a Z-Ring and the Pokemon in front is carrying a Z-Crystal with something in its moveset to convert",
      "and BURST once the trainer has a Z-Ring, the Pokemon in front is a Necrozma already fused into Dusk Mane or Dawn Wings, and it is holding Ultranecrozium Z",
      "a Pokemon can hold a mega stone, an orb or a Z-Crystal but never two of them, which was already true of a stone and an orb and now costs a real choice: giving a Charizard a crystal takes its mega stone away",
      "Ultranecrozium Z goes through that same one held-item slot, so a Necrozma holding it cannot also hold a type Z-Crystal and is never offered a Z-Move -- the crystal converts nothing on its own",
      "arming Dynamax or a Z-Move on the menu cell now changes the Pokemon's moves straight away, so the Max Move or the Z-Move can be picked on that same turn instead of the next one; pressing A on the cell again, or cycling to another transformation, puts the original moves back",
      "one transformation the player asks for per battle, across all of them: using a mega or a Dynamax now takes the menu cell away for the rest of that battle instead of leaving the other on it, which is how the mainline games have ruled the trainer's gimmick; the automatic forms are not part of it",
    },
    added = {
      "a form change that resolves before turn order, so megaing never spends a turn or a move",
      "mega stones as bag items; using one on a mon it fits assigns it to that mon and is not used up",
      "95 mega forms across 86 species, each sold under its own stone; MEGA EVOLUTIONS picks between the 47 the real games have, which is the default, and all of them -- Mega Rayquaza is the real games' 48th and needs no stone at all, so it is not among them (see below)",
      "primal reversion for Groudon and Kyogre, which happens by itself on entering battle and on switching in -- no menu entry, nothing to arm, and no once-per-battle limit, so both can revert in one fight and neither spends the trainer's one transformation",
      "the Red Orb and the Blue Orb as bag items, assigned to a Pokemon the way a stone is and sold at the Indigo Plateau lobby counter",
      "the Key Stone, the Dynamax Band and the Tera Orb as trainer key items, sold for 200 each at the head of the Celadon department store's stone shelf; each gates only its own mechanic, none is used on a Pokemon, and none can be sold, tossed or left in the PC once bought",
      "Terastallization for every species: the Pokemon becomes a single type for the rest of the battle, taking damage as that type and getting the same-type bonus on it, and it survives switching out where a Dynamax does not",
      "TERA TYPE, which picks the type a Terastallization uses; it is read at the moment the change happens, so it can be changed between battles from the mod manager",
      "TERA BLAST, which plays as an ordinary Normal-type attack until its Pokemon terastallizes and then substitutes to the chosen type on arming, following the Pokemon back in on every switch",
      "TM171, sold on the Celadon department store's stone floor right behind the Tera Orb, which teaches TERA BLAST to any Pokemon that is not a mega, Gigantamax or other alternate-form entry -- the fix for TERA BLAST having shipped with nothing able to learn it",
      "the form survives switching out and unwinds when the battle ends or the mon faints",
      "condition-driven forms for eight species, with no item and no menu: Darmanitan and Minior flip at half HP, Wishiwashi schools above a quarter HP from level 20, Aegislash draws its blade on an attacking move and shields on a status one, Morpeko alternates every round, Mimikyu and Eiscue break on a damaging hit, and Greninja bonds on a knockout it dealt",
      "the reversible ones flip back and forth as many times as the battle calls for, because the form is re-derived from the mon rather than remembered",
      "the menu cell cycles on LEFT and RIGHT when more than one transformation is available, and the trainer picks one of them for the battle -- using either takes the cell away for the rest of the fight",
      "Dynamax for every species, once per battle per trainer, lasting three turns and ending early on switching out or fainting",
      "Dynamax multiplies effective HP the way the real games do, without ever writing mon.hp or mon.stats.hp -- both are save data with no battle-only copy behind them -- by halving incoming damage against the Dynamaxed Pokemon instead, which survives exactly as long as doubling its HP would have; OHKO moves fail outright against a Dynamaxed target, matching the real games, and Super Fang needs no change because it already halves the target's current HP directly rather than dealing a fixed amount",
      "Max Moves: while a Pokemon is Dynamaxed its damaging moves become the Max Move for their own type, at a power taken from the move being replaced, and each one spends the PP of the slot it stands in for -- they are there from the turn the cell is armed, so all three of a Dynamax's turns are Max Move turns, and the moveset comes back exactly as it was on every path the Dynamax ends on",
      "MAX GUARD in place of a status move, which shields the Pokemon for the turn and moves first so that it can",
      "Z-Moves: every damaging move of a held Z-Crystal's type becomes that type's Z-Move for one use, at a power taken from the move being replaced and spending that move's PP; it is there to pick on the turn it is armed, and the moveset comes back at the end of the turn one of them is used",
      "the Z-Ring as a fourth trainer key item, and eighteen Z-Crystals -- one per type -- as bag items used on a Pokemon the way a mega stone is, sold behind the key items on the Celadon stone floor",
      "fourteen species-specific Z-Crystals, each restricted to one species and one named move, sold behind Ultranecrozium Z and sharing the same Z-MOVE cell and once-per-battle lock as the eighteen type crystals",
      "a status move that already raises the user's own stat, used under a matching Z-Crystal, additionally raises every other stat by one stage -- keeping its own effect exactly, with nothing substituted",
      "a message in the real games' own words when the Z-Power goes up, across two pages because no eighteen-character battle row holds the whole sentence",
      "Gigantamax for 31 of those species, which is a Dynamax with a form change laid on top -- the stats, the types and the picture of the G-Max form for as long as the three turns last",
      "a battle message when a Pokemon reverts or mega evolves, in the real games' own words -- neither change renames the Pokemon and primal reversion has no animation, so without a line the whole mechanic rested on one sprite being present",
      "two message pages when a Pokemon terastallizes, the second naming the type it became -- the type change is the only thing that happens, and no eighteen-character battle row holds both halves of the sentence",
      "a message at both ends of a Dynamax, because it is the only transformation here that ends on its own and a species with no Gigantamax shape has no picture change at either end to notice it by",
      "Rotom's five appliance forms, which last past the end of the battle: the five appliances are bag items sold on the Celadon stone floor and used on a Pokemon the way a mega stone is, and a Rotom given one stays in that form through the battle, the save and a reload until another appliance replaces it or the same one is used again to take it back off",
      "the appliance form is the first thing this mod writes into a save, and it writes two string fields on the Pokemon and nothing else -- the item it is stamped with, and the form marker derived from that item; the species, the stat block, current HP and the move list are all left exactly as they were",
      "fusion for Kyurem, Necrozma and Calyrex: the DNA Splicers, the N-Solarizer, the N-Lunarizer and the Reins of Unity are bag items sold at the Indigo Plateau counter beside the orbs, and using one on the base Pokemon while its partner is in the party joins the two into White or Black Kyurem, Dusk Mane or Dawn Wings Necrozma, or the Ice or Shadow Rider Calyrex",
      "using the same item again separates them and hands the partner back exactly as it went in -- same level, moves, PP, stats and DVs -- which is the only way back, the way it is in the real games",
      "the partner is deposited into the PC rather than carried inside the Pokemon it was fused with, so it stays an ordinary Pokemon that the box screen, a save validation and a Game Boy .sav export all already understand",
      "two strings are written and nothing else: which partner went in, on the survivor, and which Pokemon it is inside, on the partner -- the form is derived from the first of those, and neither half has its species, stat block, HP or move list touched",
      "the boxed partner carries a small F beside its name in the WITHDRAW and RELEASE lists and on its STATS screen, so it does not read as an ordinary Pokemon a player could release by accident -- the marker is drawn at read time and writes nothing of its own",
      "a battle already under way is picked up the moment the mod is enabled, so turning it on mid-fight from the mod manager brings the MEGA cell and primal reversion with it instead of doing nothing until the next battle",
      "DEBUG TRACE, off by default, which records into mod storage why the menu cell and primal reversion did or did not happen -- for reporting a bug, not for playing with",
      "six more persistent held-item forms, on the same mechanism as Rotom's appliances: the Griseous Orb turns Giratina into its Origin Forme, the Lustrous Globe and the Adamant Crystal do the same for Palkia and Dialga, the Rusted Sword and the Rusted Shield turn Zacian and Zamazenta Crowned, and the Gracidea puts Shaymin in Sky Forme -- all six are bag items sold at the Indigo Plateau lobby counter, used on a Pokemon the way a mega stone is, and using the same item again takes the form back off",
      "Ultra Burst for Necrozma: Ultranecrozium Z, a nineteenth Z-Crystal sold behind the other eighteen on the Celadon stone floor, turns a Necrozma already fused into Dusk Mane or Dawn Wings into Ultra Necrozma for the rest of the battle once the trainer's Z-Ring is on -- no turn, move or PP spent, one of the trainer's one manual transformation a battle, and it survives switching out the way a mega does",
      "reverting an Ultra Necrozma -- on fainting or at the battle's end -- lands back on whichever fused form it burst from rather than on a plain Necrozma, because the fusion itself never stopped being true underneath it",
      "Arceus's seventeen Plates and Silvally's seventeen Memories, on the same held-item mechanism as Rotom's appliances and the six forms beside them: the seventeen Plates turn Arceus into its matching type and the seventeen Memories do the same for Silvally, both sold at the Indigo Plateau lobby counter and used on a Pokemon the way a mega stone is, and using the same item again takes the form back off",
      "Genesect's four Drives -- Douse, Shock, Burn and Chill -- on that same held-item mechanism and sold at that same counter, behind the Memories; unlike every other family on this list a Drive changes ONLY Genesect's picture, because the real games only ever change the type of Techno Blast, a per-move property no form record here can hold, so using one carries no stat or type difference of any kind",
      "Dragon Ascent as a real move -- 120-power Flying, physical, lowering the user's own Defense and Special Defense one stage after it hits -- and Rayquaza's real mega trigger: knowing it reaches the MEGA cell with no Key Stone and no mega stone at all, refused only by a held Z-Crystal, matching the real games; every other mega still needs both items",
      "on Gold: the persistent held-item forms sold and usable for the first time -- Rotom's five appliances at Celadon department store's 4F, and the Griseous Orb, the Lustrous Globe, the Adamant Crystal, the Rusted Sword, the Rusted Shield, the Gracidea, the seventeen Plates, the seventeen Memories and the four Drives at the Indigo Plateau Pokemon Center counter, both shelves stocked by patching the mart screen's own item list rather than through a registry, since neither of Gold's mart registries has a Gen 2 home to write through",
      "on Gold: these same items now change a Pokemon's form from genuinely being HELD -- the party ITEM row's real GIVE, Gold's own held-item slot -- rather than only from a bag USE that stamped a mod-only marker; giving a Rotom a different appliance than the one it was last stamped with now wins, matching what the ITEM row itself says the Pokemon is carrying",
    },
    known = {
      "almost everything on this list needs National Dex's own NATIONAL DEX option turned ON, not merely National Dex installed and enabled: every species and form pseudo-record beyond Kanto's own 151 -- every mega, every primal, every persistent held-item form, both fusions, Ultra Burst, and eleven of the fourteen species Z-Crystals' own species -- lives in National Dex's own generated national.lua, which its main.lua loads only when that option reads ON, and it defaults to OFF; with it left off, this mod still installs cleanly and still sells its items, but almost none of them has a species left to apply to",
      "the exceptions, which work with NATIONAL DEX left off because their species are Kanto natives already on the cart: Pikashunium Z (Pikachu), Snorlium Z (Snorlax) and Mewnium Z (Mew) among the species Z-Crystals, and nothing else on this list -- every mega form, including a Kanto native's own (Mega Charizard X among them), is itself a form pseudo-record inside national.lua and needs the option too",
      "abilities are unaffected -- nothing here makes an ability do anything, and no ability animations ship yet",
      "a primal Groudon or Kyogre gets the form's stats, types and picture but not its weather, since abilities are untouched: Desolate Land and Primordial Sea do nothing here",
      "a Pokemon holds one of these items at a time -- a stone, an orb or a Z-Crystal -- the way a held item slot would work; that cost nothing while there were only stones and orbs, since no species has both a mega and a primal form, and it is a real choice now that any Pokemon can carry a crystal instead",
      "Gold now supports the persistent held-item forms alone -- Rotom's five appliances (sold on Celadon department store's 4F) and the six other held-item forms plus the Plates, Memories and Drives (sold at the Indigo Plateau Pokemon Center counter), all reachable and confirmed in a real Gold game as of 0.38.0; every other mechanic on this list -- mega evolution, primal reversion, Dynamax, Terastallization, Z-Moves, fusion, Ultra Burst, the condition-driven forms -- has no menu cell and no shop stock on Gold yet and stays Red/Blue/Yellow only",
      "on Gold, holding one of these items (the party ITEM row's GIVE) is the only trigger; using one from the bag is not offered there at all -- confirmed as of 0.39.0 that Gold's own PACK dispatcher (Game2:usePartyItem) cannot reach a mod's item, on any item, regardless of what a mod registers, so the USE verb is withdrawn from these items' menus on Gold rather than left on screen doing nothing",
      "a mon exported to a Game Boy .sav loses its stone, because a cart save has nowhere to record one -- and a Rotom loses its appliance form with it, coming back a plain Rotom that has forgotten which appliance it was in; it can never come back as a different Pokemon or as a base form carrying a form's stats, because neither the species nor the stat block is ever written",
      "primal reversion has no trainer key item, matching the real games, where the Red and Blue Orbs are the whole requirement -- so a Groudon or Kyogre reverts with an empty bag behind it",
      "Rayquazite is withdrawn as of 0.30.0, now that wild_forms places a wild Rayquaza: knowing Dragon Ascent is Rayquaza's only mega trigger, matching the real games exactly, with no item involved at all; a Rayquazite already in a bag from an earlier version stays there, inert, rather than being taken away, and its bag byte is retired permanently rather than reused for a different stone",
      "turning MEGA EVOLUTIONS down to OFFICIAL leaves any other stone already in the bag exactly where it is, inert -- a stone is never unregistered, because an item a save carries has to stay nameable",
      "every form here except the persistent ones lasts the battle and is never written to the save; Rotom's appliances and the six held-item forms beside them are the exception and are derived from the item on the Pokemon rather than remembered, so the battle-end sweep writes whatever that item vouches for and deletes everything else",
      "a persistent form or a fusion changes the Pokemon's picture, its types and its stat numbers everywhere the mod draws them -- in battle, on the party menu's STATS screen, and on the same screen reached from either PC box list -- recomputed from the form's own record at draw time rather than cached onto the Pokemon; only the name stays the base species' own, because the real games keep it too (Giratina in Origin Forme is still called Giratina) and mon.species itself is never rewritten",
      "a Pokemon holds one of these items at a time, so a Giratina carrying the Griseous Orb is a Giratina not carrying a Z-Crystal; giving it one takes Origin Forme off, which is the other way back to a plain Giratina, exactly as it is for Rotom and an appliance",
      "Shaymin's Sky Forme also wants daytime in the real games, which Gen 1 has no time-of-day system to ask about -- it is wired on the Gracidea alone rather than left out, the same liberty every other item-gated mechanic here already takes to fit this engine",
      "none of the 34 Plates and Memories carries a bag byte, unlike every other item this mod sells: the shared single-byte item space (Gen 1 stores an item as one byte, 0-255) had 22 free and the two families needed 34, and splitting them so some carry a byte and others do not had no principled line to draw it on -- so a Plate or a Memory buys, stamps and holds its form on every save this mod supports exactly like any other held item, but does not survive an export to a Game Boy .sav cartridge, where it is silently dropped from the bag the way an unrecognised item already is",
      "a Drive is cosmetic only: Genesect's own type stays BUG/STEEL and every stat holds the base form's numbers whichever Drive is held, matching what the real games actually change (Techno Blast's type, a per-move property this engine has no way to model per form) rather than implying a stat or type effect that does not exist",
      "regional forms and cosmetic forms besides Genesect's Drives are not implemented",
      "Ultra Burst needs the fusion to already exist -- fusion items are usable only outside battle, so there is no path that fuses and bursts in the same fight; the trainer fuses Necrozma first, in an earlier battle or the overworld, and carries Ultranecrozium Z into the next one",
      "a fused pair exported to a Game Boy .sav comes back as two plain Pokemon -- the base in the party, the partner in the box it was put in -- because the cartridge mon struct has no room for either of the two strings a fusion writes; nothing is lost, and the way back is to fuse them again",
      "the partner is visible in the PC while it is fused, and it can be withdrawn or released like any other boxed Pokemon: withdrawing it leaves the player holding both halves at once, and releasing it means a later separation returns nothing and says so",
      "fusing refuses, without moving anything, when there is no partner in the party, when it would leave the party with no Pokemon able to battle, and when all 240 PC slots are full; separating refuses when the party is full",
      "where one item has two possible partners -- Kyurem with both Reshiram and Zekrom, Calyrex with both Glastrier and Spectrier -- the first in party order is the one taken, so reordering the party is how the other is chosen; Necrozma needs no such rule, because its two forms have an item each",
      "a fusion has no animation: the engine's evolution screen re-keys the Pokemon's species and recomputes its stats, which is the one write this mod has refused since 0.2.1, so the item prints its message the way every other item here does",
      "a Terastallization writes nothing to the Pokemon whatsoever: the type override lives on the battler, which the battle takes with it, so there is nothing for the party sweep to find",
      "Terastallization keeps the same-type bonus only on the type it became, where the real games leave the Pokemon its original types' bonus as well",
      "the Tera Orb is never used up and needs no recharge, where the real games exhaust it until the next Pokemon Center -- there is one type on offer at a time and nothing to ration it against",
      "the three modern types are on the TERA TYPE list but resolve only where National Dex has registered a chart carrying them; picking one the running game has no record for leaves the cell absent and says so in the log",
      "Tera Blast is built as a move-substitution, not a form or a second type-override system: National Dex registers the move itself (Normal, 80 power, Special, 100 accuracy), and arming TERA substitutes that one slot for the chosen type's own variant, exactly the mechanism Max Moves and Z-Moves already use -- a Pokemon that knows it but never terastallizes plays the ordinary Normal-type attack",
      "unlike a Max Move or a Z-Move, TERA BLAST's substitution follows the Pokemon back in on every switch rather than ending on one, because Terastallization itself does",
      "one National Dex mega, Meowstic (female), has no art in the installed sprite sets and is not wired -- it would show the ordinary Meowstic sprite with a mega's stats",
      "the condition-driven forms are gated on the species, not on the ability that drives them in the real games, because a Gen 1 Pokemon has no ability field to read -- so every Darmanitan enters Zen Mode and every Greninja bonds, where those are hidden abilities only some of them would have",
      "Mimikyu's disguise and Eiscue's face change the form but do not absorb the hit that breaks them, and neither comes back during the battle",
      "Aegislash returns to its shield form on any status move, standing in for King's Shield, which Gen 1 has no move record for",
      "Castform and Cherrim are not wired: both change with the weather, and Gen 1 battles have no weather to change with",
      "Cramorant is not wired either -- Gulp Missile is a payload fired back at an attacker rather than a form, and nothing here makes an ability do anything",
      "a species can have one condition-driven form, so there is no priority order to get wrong; the reversible ones are re-derived, not tracked, and unwind with everything else at the end of the battle",
      "the condition-driven forms print no message, on purpose: several of them change every single turn, and a line each would bury the battle text a player is reading",
      "G-Max Moves are not implemented, and not for want of wiring: nothing in the species data, the move data or the engine describes one, so a Gigantamax Pokemon uses the ordinary Max Move for each of its types rather than a name with an invented power behind it",
      "a Max Move can miss, where the real games' cannot: a move record has no never-miss field to set, so they are registered at 100 accuracy and the faithful ruleset still keeps the cart's one-in-256 miss",
      "MAX GUARD borrows the engine's semi-invulnerability, which is the only thing in Gen 1 that makes an incoming move fail -- so a never-miss move goes through it, and it never fails for being used twice running the way the real games' does",
      "a move whose type this game's chart has no record for keeps itself while Dynamaxed, which in practice means the three modern types in a game where National Dex has registered no chart carrying them",
      "an armed Z-Move that is never used simply stays: there is no clock to run out, so the substituted moveset stands until one of its moves is spent, the Pokemon switches or faints, or the battle ends -- or until the player takes it back off by pressing A on the cell a second time or cycling away from it",
      "fourteen species-specific Z-Moves are built, each behind its own crystal restricted to one species (or a small family of forms of it) and one named move -- Catastropika, Stoked Sparksurfer, Pulverizing Pancake, Genesis Supernova, Sinister Arrow Raid, Malicious Moonsault, Oceanic Operetta, Splintered Stormshards, Let's Snuggle Forever, Clangorous Soulblaze, Searing Sunraze Smash, Menacing Moonraze Maelstrom, Soul-Stealing 7-Star Strike and 10,000,000 Volt Thunderbolt -- refused outright before this on the grounds that every one of them keyed off a base move Gen 1 did not have, which stopped being true once National Dex registered all 833 modern moves",
      "of those fourteen, thirteen are actually reachable as of 0.36.0 -- National Dex registers Volt Tackle, Giga Impact, Darkest Lariat, Sparkling Aria, Stone Edge, Play Rough, Clanging Scales, Sunsteel Strike, Moongeist Beam and Spectral Thief with `effectModeled = false`, which permanently bars all ten from any learnset it builds, so nothing could learn them until src/speciesbasemoves.lua modelled each one's real effect and taught it directly the way Dragon Ascent and TM171 already were; Sunsteel Strike and Moongeist Beam are taught as plain damage, since their whole effect past that is ignoring an ability and this engine has none",
      "Decidium Z (Spirit Shackle, Decidueye) is the one exception and is refused rather than faked: Spirit Shackle's real effect beyond damage prevents the target switching out, and this engine has no seam a mod can reach to block a switch decision without changing engine code, so it stays exactly as unreachable as every one of the fourteen was before 0.36.0",
      "Guardian of Alola and Extreme Evoboost are still left out of that roster, for reasons that have nothing to do with the byte ceiling: Guardian of Alola computes its damage from the target's current HP rather than a fixed power, and Extreme Evoboost replaces its move with a pure stat boost and deals no damage at all, both a different shape from the fourteen this build covers rather than more of the same",
      "10,000,000 Volt Thunderbolt is gated on Pikachu's eight cosmetic cap forms, matching the real games, rather than loosened to work on an ordinary Pikachu, which already has its own crystal in Pikashunium Z",
      "a status move under a crystal of its own type still keeps itself rather than becoming a move, and now also raises every OTHER stat by one stage if its own effect already raises one of the user's own -- the one shape of the real games' Z-status bonus table this build covers; every other status move still keeps only itself, since the rest of that table would mean inventing a ruling this codebase has no source to check against",
      "the Z-Move names printed in battle text and Mimic are the move data's own, unshortened spelling; thirteen of the eighteen are longer than a Gen 1 move name, so the FIGHT menu itself shows a shorter display name instead -- measured to fit both the classic and the widescreen layout -- rather than truncating or running into the box border",
      "a Z-Move can miss for the same reason a Max Move can: a move record has no never-miss field, so they are registered at 100 accuracy and the faithful ruleset keeps the cart's one-in-256 miss",
      "the Z-Ring is never used up and there is nothing to recharge, and a crystal is kept rather than consumed -- the once-per-battle limit is the only thing rationing either",
      "there is no power spot: the Dynamax Band and the menu cell are the whole trigger, so a trainer carrying one can Dynamax anywhere, where the real games also gate it on where the battle is happening",
      "three species with a Gigantamax form are not wired -- Corviknight, and the Low Key and Rapid Strike variants of Toxtricity and Urshifu -- because there is no usable art for them; all three still Dynamax plainly",
      "a Dynamax laid over a form some other mechanic applied -- a primal reversion, or a condition-driven form -- leaves that form alone at both ends: no Gigantamax shape goes on over it, and the three turns running out does not strip what the Dynamax never applied",
      "enabling the mod mid-battle brings across only what is still derivable -- primal reversion and the HP-driven forms -- so an Aegislash or a Mimikyu whose trigger already fired arrives in its base form and resumes from the next one, and the turn already in progress cannot be megaed retroactively",
    },
  },
  credits = {
    { who = "National Dex", for_ = "the alternate-form records every form change reads its stats and types from" },
  },
  compat = { engine = ">=0.0.0-0 <2.0.0", modApi = 2 },
}
